spec: specs/optics-outside.md (stage 5)

why: the operator read the declaration form four times and asked for
  something shorter each time. Every proposal was tried in the
  compiler rather than argued about, and the outcomes decided the
  design — including two that were REFUSED by the language and one
  that was refused by measurement.

what, all settled by probe (scratchpad/probe/*.scala):
  - `:?` becomes chainable, so `+&` leaves the common case and keeps
    only its real job: factoring a shared query value out.
  - an OPTIONAL syntax behind an explicit import: `"id"[Int]` in a
    path, `"q"[String]` / `"page".opt[Int]` / `"tag".all[String]` in a
    query. A bare string is a literal segment, a typed string is a
    hole. `Route[Int]("id")` and `Query[String]("q")` stay, because
    they name the concepts aloud.
  - a path may not follow a query, STRUCTURALLY: `:?` answers a
    `Queried[A]`, which has no `/`. `Routed[A]` carries what both can
    do (unapply, url, describe, prism, of) and `Router` takes that.
  - `Router.on` and `Toolbox.on` on the companions, so a table is not
    started with `.empty.`; `empty` stays as the zero of a fold.

refused, and recorded: `:?[String]("q")` and `:?[String] "q"` do not
  parse — Scala takes precedence from an operator's FIRST character
  and `[` may not follow one. `Router./` as a name for the empty
  table is declined: `/` already means "append a path segment" here.

and a finding worth its own note: `@compileTimeOnly` on a poisoned
  `Named./` was tried to improve the refusal MESSAGE. It fired in five
  isolated variants and silently did not in the real expression, even
  after a clean rebuild — and its presence made two invalid
  declarations COMPILE. Not used; the structural refusal does not
  depend on it.

next: spec stage 5, then Route/Queried, then the syntax object, then
  the companions, then docs
